     1        23                     ! WARNING WARNING WARNING WARNING WARNING WARNING WARNING WARNING WARNING
     1        24                     !!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!
     1        25                     !
     1        26                     ! Do ***not*** copy this file to the directory where your Fortran
     1        27                     ! fortran application is compiled unless it is absolutely necessary!  Most
     1        28                     ! modern Fortran compilers now support the -I command line flag, which
     1        29                     ! tells the compiler where to find .h files (specifically, this one).  For
     1        30                     ! example:
     1        31                     !
     1        32                     !      shell$ mpifort foo.f -o foo -I$OMPI_HOME/include
     1        33                     !
     1        34                     ! will probably do the trick (assuming that you have set OMPI_HOME
     1        35                     ! properly).
     1        36                     !
     1        37                     ! That being said, OMPI's "mpifort" wrapper compiler should
     1        38                     ! automatically include the -I option for you.  The following command
     1        39                     ! should be equivalent to the command listed above:
     1        40                     !
     1        41                     !      shell$ mpifort foo.f -o foo
     1        42                     !
     1        43                     ! You should not copy this file to your local directory because it is
     1        44                     ! possible that this file will be changed between versions of Open MPI.
     1        45                     ! Indeed, this mpif.h is incompatible with the mpif.f of other
     1        46                     ! implementations of MPI.  Using this mpif.h with other implementations
     1        47                     ! of MPI, or with other versions of Open MPI will result in undefined
     1        48                     ! behavior (to include incorrect results, segmentation faults,
     1        49                     ! unexplainable "hanging" in your application, etc.).  Always use the
     1        50                     ! -I command line option instead (or let mpifort do it for you).
     1        51                     !
